trace_interval was used (widened by 2^-280) to construct the Newton box
directly, so trace_interval subset box by construction.

The disjointness argument, made explicit:
  (1) qa is irreducible over Q (verified, Step 2) and Q has
      characteristic 0, so qa is automatically SEPARABLE: an
      irreducible polynomial sharing a repeated root with itself
      would share a nonzero common factor with its own derivative,
      contradicting irreducibility (the derivative cannot vanish
      identically in characteristic 0 for a nonconstant polynomial).
      Hence qa, having degree 10, has EXACTLY 10 DISTINCT complex roots.
  (2) STEP 4's interval-Newton contraction rigorously certifies
      that EXACTLY ONE of those 10 roots lies in box.
  (3) By (1) and (2), the remaining 9 roots -- wherever they are --
      are, by elementary counting, NOT in box. This requires no
      numerical information about their locations at all.

qa.degree() == 10: True
qa.is_squarefree() (exact, formal separability certificate): True

UNIQUENESS CERTIFIED: exactly one qa root lies in the box containing
the certified trace interval. Disjointness from the other 9 roots
follows from uniqueness + separability + degree count, not from
individual root enclosures.

